// @emit-error unsupported .map/.filter
// THE DECLINE, PINNED — and it replaced a check-clean invalid module, which is why it is an
// improvement rather than a limitation.
//
// An inline literal-union callback return (`xs.map((v: i32): "a" | "b" => …)`). The canon pass
// softens the inline union to `string`, so `cloRetIsString` — which read the annotation SPELLING
// — answered TRUE and the result was built with the string-list kind while its elements are
// interned i32 ATOMS. `vl check --codegen` exited 0 and the module failed to validate:
// `type mismatch: expected i32, found (ref $type)`.
//
// Reading the ARENA instead keeps the `TyUnion` of `TyLit`s the spelling softened away, so the
// answer is FALSE and the shape reaches this loud decline at check time.
//
// THE REAL FIX IS SUPPORT, NOT REJECTION. The plain-function spelling of the same type already
// works (`literal-unions/inline-atom.vl`: `function pick(): "x" | "y" { return "x" }`), so there
// is no reason in the language for the callback position to differ — only in the `.map`/`.filter`
// result classifier, which has no atom-list result kind. When that lands, this file becomes an
// `@run` asserting `a`.
//
// THE WITNESS HAD TO BE CONSTRUCTED. The corpus contains exactly ONE inline-litunion return
// annotation, on a plain function, never on a callback — so no sweep could have found this, and
// the conversion that exposed it measured 0 corpus rows.
const xs = [1, 2]
const ys = xs.map((v: i32): "a" | "b" => {
  return "a"
})
print(ys[0])
